  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe limiting factor in current optimization approaches is the sensitivity to local optima in high-dimensional search spaces. This is addressed by introducing a multi-resolution strategy that combines global exploration with targeted optimization. The core idea is to:\n1. Start with multiple low-resolution initial conditions (N=64, 128) that can be quickly evaluated\n2. Reconstruct these on the target resolution (N=560) to inherit shape properties\n3. Use asymmetric transfer learning from single-grid optimization to recover shape-adaptive information\n4. Combine with randomized local searches at fine resolution\n\nThis hybrid approach balances exploration across resolutions with exploitation at the target scale, specifically targeting configurations that minimize maximum shift correlations. The mathematical foundation draws from random shift invariance properties of periodic functions while maintaining discrete structure.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Representation: Piecewise-constant step functions controlled by 6-bit quantizations (49152 patterns)\n- Objective: Minimize C_5 = max_{k=-N+1}^{N-1} \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1, \u222bh(x)dx=1\n- Objective formulation: The full correlation includes both forward and backward shifts with weights X-length scaling\n- Search space: Constructed from 4 orthogonal basis sets:\n  - 8 periodic sinusoidal components\n  - 12 gradient-anti-correlated patterns\n  - 10 localized peak configurations\n\nImplementation procedure:\n\n1. Prefix kernel construction:\n   - Initialize 3 candidates on N=64: periodic, chaotic-wave, sparse bursts\n   - For each:\n        * Fully optimize on N=64 using p-norm method\n        * Reconstruct to N=560 via interpolation (ensuring integral preservation)\n        * Run asymmetric L-BFGS refinement (gradient-only constraint handling)\n\n2. Phase 1: Initial assessment:\n   - Input candidates: repaired_64 + initial_560 + randomWave_560\n   - Output: Best candidate (C_5, score)\n\n3. Phase 2: Asymmetric refinement:\n   - For each candidate group at resolution 560:\n        * Perform 1000 L-BFGS steps with capped iteration count\n        * Apply constrained optimization with:\n            - Integral constraint projection\n            - Box constraint maintenance\n        * Exit if time budget < 100s remaining\n\n4. Phase 3: Periodic shifting simulation:\n   - Generate shift-invariant mask by:\n        * Computing autocorrelation at degrees 0-7\n        * Selecting shifts that maximize deviation\n   - Apply shift-invariant repair on any candidate with C_5 > 0.39\n\n5. Break conditions:\n   - Time limit: 550s execution time\n   - No improvement for 200s\n   - Three consecutive non-improving candidates\n\nSearch choices:\n- Candidate generation: \n   periodic: h(x) = 0.5(1+cos(2\u03c0xf)), with f\u22480.25 for 64-point grid\n   chaotic-wave: combination of modified sine waves at frequencies 0.15, 0.22\n   sparse bursts: clustered Gaussian-like structures\n- Optimization hyperparams:\n   - L-BFGS: m=20, \u03c1=0.1, \u03b5=1e-8\n   - Projections: adaptive target cut (50-3609)\n- Resolution progression:\n   - 64 \u2192 128 \u2192 560\n   - Interpolation type: Cubic spline with integral preservation\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n- N=64 optimization: ~80s\n- N=560 adaptation: ~500s\n- Timers:\n   - Reconstruct: manual verification on subset\n   - Syncronize: clock-adjusted threads\n- Local fallback:\n   ```python\n   if not_improved_for(time.duration*0.2):\n       return best_candidate_scaled(current, N/2)\n   ```\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Check candidate: 0<=h[i]<=1\u2200i, \u222bh\u22480.5 (with tolerance 1e-5)\n2. Recompute C_5 separately to catch floating point errors\n3. Verify objective value against parent method\n4. Small-test: For any candidate, verify \n   C_5_reference\u2248C_5_fast (within 1e-6)\n\nThe plan directly addresses the integer program structure in terms of shift-invariance while exploiting periodic patterns. The key implementation details leverage dimensionality reduction between resolutions and targeted attacks on the autocorrelation evaluation.",
